TESTDATA["pet5-t1"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 5 · Test 1","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"COLLEGE STAFF/STUDENT\nStudents cannot get on the bus without ID cards.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["This bus service cannot be used by college staff unless they show ID cards.","Students can get their ID cards on the bus.","Students are not allowed on the bus unless they have ID cards."],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"text":"To: Jane  From: Laura\nThe jumper's lovely – thanks. Could you tell me where you got it? I'd like to buy another one to match the coat I also got for my birthday.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Laura liked the jumper Jane bought, but needs it in a different size.","Laura wants to try to get the same jumper in a different colour.","Laura received two jumpers which were the same, so wants to exchange one."],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"text":"UNIVERSITY HOLIDAYS\nFrom next Friday, the library will be closed during weekends and evenings.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"The library will","options":["have shorter opening hours until next Friday.","change its opening hours next Friday.","open again to students next Friday."],"answer":"B"},{"n":4,"text":"Mark,\nWe went on a bus sightseeing tour of the city yesterday. We didn't stop anywhere but saw more than you would on foot.\nJo","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Jo is pleased with the number of things she saw from the bus.","Jo regrets not having walked around the city to look at the sights.","Jo thinks there are better sightseeing tours than the one she took."],"answer":"A"},{"n":5,"text":"Market vehicles unload here 07.00–10.00 daily. Customer parking at other times","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Customers may park outside the market for up to three hours.","You may unload your vehicle here at any time.","Customers may park here at times when vehicles are not unloading."],"answer":"C"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Cristina","text":"Cristina wants to visit a museum with her daughter to see how people used to live. They want to have lunch there and buy some presents to take home. They will go by car.","answer":"E"},{"n":7,"name":"Joanne","text":"Joanne wants to visit a museum and see people making things. She has no car and would like to have lunch there.","answer":"C"},{"n":8,"name":"Carl","text":"Carl's hobby is painting pictures, so he would like to see the work of other painters who live in the area. He wants to have a snack at the museum. He travels by public transport.","answer":"D"},{"n":9,"name":"Duncan","text":"Duncan wants to find out where local people worked in the past and what they did in their spare time. He wants to buy a book about the exhibition. He travels by bus.","answer":"B"},{"n":10,"name":"The Cannavaro family","text":"The Cannavaro family want to walk around a site which offers opportunities for the whole family to take part in activities. They want somewhere pleasant to eat their own sandwiches. They have a car.","answer":"G"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"Stackworth Museum","text":"Stackworth Museum tells the history of the famous Stackworth family, and gives information about other well-known local people. These include poets, artists and writers. There is an excellent café and a car park."},{"letter":"B","title":"Charberth Museum","text":"Charberth Museum is near the main bus station and has a rich collection of objects, 19th-century paintings and photographs showing life in the town over the centuries — the jobs people did and how they entertained themselves. An accompanying book showing the works on display is available from the Museum Shop as well as some attractive gifts. There is no café."},{"letter":"C","title":"Fairley Museum","text":"Fairley Museum is arranged like an old-fashioned village. You can see people working at their trades to produce tools, pots and even boats using traditional skills. There is a small picnic area in the car park but most people eat in the excellent café. The museum is on a bus route."},{"letter":"D","title":"Westerleigh Museum","text":"Westerleigh Museum is near the bus station and contains exhibitions showing the town's development. In a separate room there are works by some well-known artists as well as changing exhibitions of work by local artists. Sandwiches, cakes and hot drinks are on sale in the café."},{"letter":"E","title":"Scotwood Manor","text":"The rooms in Scotwood Manor are furnished as they were 100 years ago. The staff spend the day as people did then and are happy to explain what it was like. There are activity sheets for children and a shop with books, souvenirs and cards, as well as a good café and car park."},{"letter":"F","title":"Freshwater Museum","text":"Freshwater was once an important fishing port. Freshwater Museum, inside the harbour office, shows how the town developed and later became a tourist centre. There is an activity room for young children with DVDs, a large picnic area, car park, and good bus service."},{"letter":"G","title":"Woodlands Museum","text":"Set in beautiful countryside, the Woodlands Museum is arranged like a village of 100 years ago. To learn more about this period, visitors are encouraged to spend time doing practical things such as making pots and cooking. There is an adventure playground with a picnic area under the trees and parking."},{"letter":"H","title":"Middleworth Museum","text":"Middleworth Museum is full of objects from the past, which tell the story of different people who worked in the area, from factory workers to the men who built the canal and railway. There is a Family Folder of things to do. The museum has a café and is near the bus and railway station."}]},{"part":3,"title":"The Short Story Society","text":"The Short Story Society exists to help writers in Britain today. Started in 1899, the Short Story Society is now one of Britain's most active arts organisations.\n\nSociety Magazines\nWe produce a range of excellent magazines, including the popular Short Story Review. This magazine includes short stories by some of Britain's top writers, as well as providing plenty of opportunities for new writers to have their work printed. Another magazine, Short Story News, has interviews with writers and is packed with information about events, competitions, festivals and the writing scene in general. To see a selection of articles from recent issues, follow the links on this website.\n\nNational Short Story Competition\nWe run the National Short Story Competition, the biggest competition of its kind. It is open to anyone aged 18 and over and short stories on any topic are accepted. Short stories should be between 1,000 and 1,500 words long. There is no entry fee for anyone belonging to the Society (non-members pay an entry fee of £5). The competition runs from April until the end of October each year. The ten best entries will appear in the Society's annual book of short stories. The actual winner will receive a publishing contract to produce his or her own collection of short stories.\n\nEducation\nFor nearly thirty years the Society has taken writers into classrooms, providing children and teachers with new ideas and building confidence in their own writing. Current projects include the Young Writer of the Year, which was started in 1998 and is open to writers aged 11–18. All winners receive book prizes and are invited to study on a five-day course taught by professional writers.\n\nThe Writers' Café\nThe Writers' Café is the social centre of the Society. Relaxed and stylish, with freshly cooked vegetarian food, excellent coffees and cold drinks, it is the ideal place to look through the Society's magazines. The Café also provides monthly exhibitions, short courses and readings. Its location is in the heart of London, and it is open from 11.00 am to 10.00 pm Monday to Friday and 11.00 am to 8.00 pm on Saturday. Society members receive discounts on selected products and events.\n\nBecoming a Member\nWe have members worldwide, and anyone aged eighteen or over is welcome to join. If you are interested in joining The Short Story Society, click here and fill out a registration form.","items":[{"n":11,"q":"The Short Story Society has existed for over a century.","answer":"A"},{"n":12,"q":"Short Story Review publishes work by inexperienced writers.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"Articles from one of the Society's magazines are available on the internet.","answer":"A"},{"n":14,"q":"Writers entering the National Short Story Competition must choose a subject suggested by the Society.","answer":"B"},{"n":15,"q":"It costs £5 for members of the Society to enter the National Short Story Competition.","answer":"B"},{"n":16,"q":"Each year, ten winning writers will be offered contracts to publish their own books.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"The Short Story Society visits schools to give pupils help with writing.","answer":"A"},{"n":18,"q":"Society members can attend regular events at the Writers' Café.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"q":"The Writers' Café is situated just outside London.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"q":"Children from anywhere in the world are able to become members of the Society.","answer":"B"}]},{"part":4,"title":"New TV Star – Caroline Benson talks about her first TV role","text":"'I never expected to spend some of my first year at university filming The Finnegans. I'd only ever acted at school, but I'd loved the book since I was eleven. My grandmother used to say I was just like Polly Finnegan and I always imagined myself playing her.\n\nI'd taken a year off to go travelling before university. While I was in Chile, my mother emailed me to say there were plans to turn the book into a TV drama. I knew I had to go for the part. She was surprised at first, but sent my photograph to the director and persuaded him to meet me. I flew back and got the part.\n\nThe outdoor filming started a week into term, so I got permission from the university to be away for three weeks. Once I was back at university, I got up at 6.00 am to write the essays I'd missed. I didn't tell my university friends, but they found out and thought it was great.\n\nIt was an amazing experience – I'm so lucky. After university, I definitely want to make acting my career. I'm not from an acting family, though my grandfather was an opera singer. I've tried for other TV parts but haven't received any offers yet.\n\nI don't know how I managed it all, because I had a full social life too. When filming finished, I hardly knew what to do. I've since appeared in two college plays. Unfortunately, I haven't been home much and now my first year at university is over, I'm off to Greece for the summer with friends.'","items":[{"n":21,"q":"In this text, Caroline Benson is","options":["advising students to finish studying before taking up acting.","describing how pleased she was about this opportunity to act.","warning other young people that acting is a difficult career.","explaining why she has always wanted to be an actor."],"answer":"B"},{"n":22,"q":"Why did Caroline decide to try for a part in The Finnegans?","options":["She thought the book would make a great TV drama.","She agreed with her grandmother that she should apply.","She felt she was perfect for the part of Polly.","She was anxious about starting university."],"answer":"C"},{"n":23,"q":"What does Caroline say about her mother?","options":["She encouraged Caroline to keep travelling.","She felt Caroline would be a good actor.","She was sorry she had emailed Caroline.","She helped Caroline to get the part."],"answer":"D"},{"n":24,"q":"How did Caroline manage to find time to do the filming?","options":["She missed lectures and hoped nobody would notice.","She delayed going to university until filming was over.","She took time off and did her college work later.","She asked her friends to help with her essays."],"answer":"C"},{"n":25,"q":"Which of the following would Caroline write to a penfriend?","options":["I'm going to continue with my studies, but hope to have the opportunity to do another TV programme soon.","Now I've finished both the filming and my first year at university, I plan to spend more time with my family.","I enjoyed filming the TV drama but I've missed having a social life – I don't know what to do at weekends.","Acting is more difficult than I'd expected, but I've learned a lot from other members of my family who work in the business."],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":5,"title":"Grass","text":"Grass is probably the (0) ……… (most) successful living plant in the world. There are over 9,000 different types of grasses and they are [26] in every region on the earth. They are the [27] flowering plants that can exist in the freezing [28] of the Arctic and the Antarctic.\nGrasslands support a wide range of animal life, from tiny insects and birds to huge animals like cows and lions. All of them [29] on grass in one way or another.\nGrass [30] very quickly after it is cut or [31]. Unlike other plants, the new leaves grow from [32] the soil, not from the top of the plant. That is [33] large families of animals are able to live together in one area. As [34] as they have eaten all the grass there, a fresh meal is always [35] because the plants start to grow again.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["noticed","realised","caught","found"],"answer":"D"},"27":{"options":["single","one","only","special"],"answer":"C"},"28":{"options":["environment","scene","situation","background"],"answer":"A"},"29":{"options":["depend","build","turn","hang"],"answer":"A"},"30":{"options":["repeats","recovers","reduces","remains"],"answer":"B"},"31":{"options":["hurt","broken","injured","damaged"],"answer":"D"},"32":{"options":["beside","behind","below","beyond"],"answer":"C"},"33":{"options":["why","where","what","when"],"answer":"A"},"34":{"options":["fast","soon","quickly","often"],"answer":"B"},"35":{"options":["available","present","free","complete"],"answer":"A"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some sentences about a sports centre.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"Sarah asked me if I was still a member of the sports centre.","before":"Sarah asked me, '","after":"still a member of the sports centre?'","answers":["are you"]},{"n":2,"first":"Non-members cannot enter the sports centre without a ticket.","before":"Non-members aren't","after":"to enter the sports centre without a ticket.","answers":["permitted","allowed","able","authorised","authorized"]},{"n":3,"first":"Trainers must be worn in the sports centre at all times.","before":"You must","after":"trainers in the sports centre at all times.","answers":["wear your","wear","be wearing your","be wearing"]},{"n":4,"first":"Football is the most popular sport at the centre.","before":"Football is","after":"than any other sport at the sports centre.","answers":["more popular"]},{"n":5,"first":"Sarah thinks the sports centre is too small for the town.","before":"Sarah thinks the sports centre is not","after":"for the town.","answers":["big enough","large enough"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"You want to borrow your English friend Sam's bicycle.\n\nWrite an email to your English friend Sam. In your email, you should\n• explain why you need to borrow the bicycle\n• say how long you will need it for\n• tell Sam when you will return it.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["spiegare perché ti serve la bicicletta","dire per quanto tempo ti serve","dire quando la restituirai"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from an English friend.\n\n'A new restaurant has just opened in my town and it's wonderful. Have you got a favourite restaurant? Tell me about the food and what you like about the restaurant.'\n\nNow write a letter to your friend about a restaurant.\nWrite your letter on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"story","task":"Your English teacher wants you to write a story.\nYour story must begin with this sentence:\n\nI was on the beach when my mobile phone rang.\n\nWrite your story on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet5-t1-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"What was damaged in the storm?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"q":"What present does the man decide to take?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"q":"Which is the woman's jacket?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":4,"q":"Which sport is not included in the price of the holiday?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":5,"q":"Which postcard will they send?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"Where do they decide to go?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":7,"q":"What will the boy do first?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"}],"example":"img/pet5-t1-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear an English woman called Britta talking to an interviewer about her life in Berlin, the capital of Germany.","audio":"audio/pet5-t1-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"How long has Britta lived in Berlin?","options":["four years","six years","twenty years"],"answer":"A"},{"n":9,"q":"What does Britta say about living in Berlin?","options":["She can't sleep at night because of the traffic noise.","She misses the museums and theatres in Bonn.","She likes living in a big, busy city."],"answer":"C"},{"n":10,"q":"The area of Berlin where Britta lives is","options":["a rather expensive place to live.","a good place to eat out.","a long way from the city centre."],"answer":"B"},{"n":11,"q":"How does Britta usually travel around in Berlin?","options":["She walks.","She uses her bicycle.","She uses the tram or bus."],"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"Britta says that her nephew, Philippe, likes going","options":["to the park with her.","to the shops with his parents.","to a gallery with her."],"answer":"C"},{"n":13,"q":"Britta has lots of friends who","options":["live near her.","work with her.","are still in England."],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a man called Stephen Mills talking to a group of people about a trip to India to see tigers.","audio":"audio/pet5-t1-l3.mp3","notes":"THE TIGER TOUR\nStephen's profession: [14]\nDate of departure: [15]\nNumber of tourists in group: [16]\nType of accommodation: [17]\nMeans of transport in the park:\n– open truck in the north\n– [18] in the south\nMeal not included: [19] on the last day","answers":{"14":["artist","painter","an artist","a painter"],"15":["6th November","6 November","6th of November","6 of November","November 6th","November 6","6/11"],"16":["18","eighteen"],"17":["hotel","hotels"],"18":["elephant","elephants"],"19":["lunch"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a conversation between a boy, Carl, and a girl, Susanna, about a school concert.","audio":"audio/pet5-t1-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"Susanna feels shy about playing her violin in public.","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"q":"Carl and Susanna share the same opinion about practising their instruments regularly.","answer":"B"},{"n":22,"q":"Susanna's parents refuse to allow her to give up violin lessons.","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"Carl's aim is to have a career in music.","answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"Susanna thinks she would enjoy working in another country.","answer":"A"},{"n":25,"q":"Carl persuades Susanna to take part in the concert.","answer":"B"}]}]};
